  "scriptId": "sim-03-fractions-of-and-complex-fractions",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Fractions, 'Of', and Complex Fractions",
    "description": "Simplify expressions built from fractions and mixed numbers, including 'of' chains, division by a fraction, and ladder fractions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start from a concrete 'of' story, build through mixed-number conversion, 'of' chains with cancellation, reciprocal division, BODMAS with fractions, stacked fractions, and ladder fractions climbed from the bottom. Each rule is demonstrated with one worked line per change, then checked with a fresh question and parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach fraction work inside simplification: converting mixed numbers to improper fractions and back, 'of' as multiplication in chains such as 2/3 of 3/4 of 96, and dividing by a fraction as multiplying by its reciprocal. Apply BODMAS to expressions mixing fractions with +, −, ×, ÷, then handle complex fractions (a fraction over a fraction, where the main bar means divide) and short ladder fractions such as 1 + 1/(2 + 1/3) worked strictly from the bottom up. Use common-denominator addition and subtraction with mixed numbers for capstone items. Show cancellation before multiplying as the speed habit exams reward. Learners should keep every intermediate value as an exact fraction and convert back to a mixed number only when the answer needs it."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn how fractions behave inside simplification.\n\nBy the end you will handle 'of' chains, mixed numbers, stacked fractions, and even ladder fractions — the exact shapes the book's 'Simplify' questions love.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn how fractions behave inside simplification.\n\nA shop sells 3/4 of its 28 chairs.\n\n'Of' with a fraction means multiply:\n\n3/4 of 28\n= 3/4 × 28\n= 3 × 7\n= 21 chairs",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Notice the shortcut: divide by the bottom number first, then multiply by the top.\n\n28 ÷ 4 = 7\n7 × 3 = 21\n\nDividing first keeps the numbers small — and small numbers mean fewer mistakes.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
